messages.body_plain:
Hi stan users,

I am working on a multi-response poisson model (counts of a number of bird species are dependent on a set of predictors through species specific coefficients/parameters) and am currently trying to optimize the code to get rid of divergent transitions etc. and to speed up the analysis. Among other things I follow the suggestions in the manual about vectorizing probability functions and vectorization through matrix operations.

The multi_normal probability functions (all versions including the cholesky variant which I am using) in Stan apparently can handle the argument Mu (location) as well as  the declared variable (left of the tilde) being an array of vectors while the scale (Sigma) is entered as a matrix. Thus, to vectorize the multi_normal sampling statement I can enter (in the model block) something like

loglambda  ~ multi_normal_cholesky(Mu, L);

where loglambda and Mu are both N length arrays of R length vectors and L is the cholesky decomposed Sigma (matrix[R,R]) with N being the number of sites in my data and R being the number of species. I can hence vectorize the whole sampling statement and do not need to loop through my sites like this:

for (n in 1:N) {
        loglambda[n] ~ multi_normal_cholesky(Mu[n], L);
}

However. I need to form Mu which is the predicted location(mean) and is dependent on the combination of my design matrix X (N by K (K=number of predictors + intercept)) and a matrix of species specific coefficients Beta (K by R). I can do this in an efficient way (says the manual) using matrix algebra:

Mu=X*Beta;

But this produces a matrix Mu (N by R) which is not (as far as I know) what multi_normal_cholesky() want. So an alternative is to use a loop to produce Mu in the right format (an N length array of R length vectors) by declaring X and Mu like:

row_vector[K] X[N];  // need to be row_vector to conform to the matrix algebra in the loop...
row_vector[R] Mu[N];  // need to be row_vector to conform to the matrix algebra in the loop...

keeping Beta as a matrix[K, R] and looping through the sites (N):

for(n in 1:N){
        Mu[n] = X[n]*Beta; // Mu[n] is a row_vector of length R
      }

But then I am not using the vectorization through matrix operations technique to the fullest?

My question is then: Is there an efficient way to "transform"(reshape) a matrix (matrix[N,R] Mu) to an array (vector[R] Mu[N]) such that it would be possible to have:

Mu=X*Beta;
"some reshaping function efficiently turning the matrix[N,R] Mu to the array vector[R] Mu[N]"
loglambda  ~ multi_normal_cholesky(Mu, L);

Or is the looping step above a minor cost in terms of efficiency such that a reshaping function would not improve anything? Are there other solutions?

I'd really appreciate any input on this

Sincerely,
Martin

Hello, I apologize in advance if my question is naive or if my code is not well-written. I am quite new to STAN. For practical reasons, I copy-paste my code below :

functions{
  vector compute_delta(vector delta_d, matrix Kxd, matrix invKd){
    return Kxd*(invKd*append_row(rep_vector(0,1),delta_d));
  }

  matrix compute_A(vector c, int Ns, int Ndim){
    vector[Ndim] E1;
    vector[Ndim] O;
    vector[Ndim] E;
    matrix[Ndim,Ns] A;
    vector[Ndim] auxA;
    vector[Ndim] auxA2;
    O = rep_vector(1, Ndim);
    E1 = rep_vector(0, Ndim);
    E1[1] = 1;
    auxA = O + sqrt(Ndim)*E1;
    for (l in 1:Ns){
      auxA2 = rep_vector(0,Ndim);
      A[,l] = rep_vector(0,Ndim);
      for (k in 1:(Ndim-1)){
        E = rep_vector(0,Ndim);
        E[k+1] = 1;
        auxA2 = auxA2 + c[k+(l-1)*(Ndim-1)]*(E - (2*(to_row_vector(auxA)*E)/(to_row_vector(auxA)*auxA))*O - (2*sqrt(Ndim)*(to_row_vector(auxA)*E)/(to_row_vector(auxA)*auxA))*E1);
      }
      A[,l] = auxA2;
    }
    return A;
  }
 }

data{
  int<lower=1> p;
  int<lower=1> Ndim;
  int<lower=1> K;
  int<lower=2> Nc;
  int<lower=1> Ns;
  real t[K];
  vector[Ndim] y[K];
  int<lower=1> group[K];
  matrix[Nc,Nc] invKd;
  matrix[Ndim,Nc] Kxd;
}

parameters{
  real<lower=0> sigma_alpha;
  real<lower=0> sigma_tau;
  real<lower=0> sigma;
  // Random effects
  real<lower=0> p0;
  real<lower=0> t0;
  real<lower=0> xi_barre;
  vector[Nc-1] delta_d;
  vector[(Ndim-1)*Ns] c;
  vector[p] alpha_raw;
  vector[p] tau_raw;
  vector[Ns] s[p];
}

transformed parameters{
  vector[Ndim] mu[K];
  vector[p] tau;
  vector<lower=0>[p] alpha;
  // Non-centered parametrization
  tau = t0 + sigma_tau*tau_raw;
  alpha = exp(xi_barre + sigma_alpha*alpha_raw);
  // Compute the means mu[j]
  for (j in 1:K) {
    mu[j] = rep_vector(p0,Ndim) + rep_vector(alpha[group[j]]*(t[j]-tau[group[j]]),Ndim) + exp(xi_barre)*compute_delta(delta_d,Kxd,invKd) + compute_A(c,Ns,Ndim)*s[group[j]];
  }
}

model{
  // Distribution of the random effects
  alpha_raw ~ normal(0,1);
  tau_raw ~ normal(0,1);
  for (j in 1:p){
    s[j] ~ normal(rep_vector(0,Ns),1);
  }
  // Likelihood
  for (j in 1:K){
    y[j] ~ normal(mu[j],sigma);
  }
}

The functions 'compute_A' and 'compute_delta' are specific to my problem. I do not think that explaining their role in the model is relevant here. Still, if you want, I can explain more.
The problem I am facing is the following one. I would like to use this STAN code with data which is such that :
* p = 700
* Ndim = 2000
* K = 3147
* Nc = 250
* Ns = 2
but after my code is compiled, I see that more than 40Gb of RAM is used on my computer. Eventually, the processes associated to STAN go into SWAP memory and I have to kill them before my computer freezes.
What am I doing wrong ? Why is this code using so much memory in less than 1 minute ? Overall, the data is approximately 40Mb large, which is not so much (from my point of view).
Is this problem due to because I have many 'transformed parameters' ?

By the way, I am using rstan_2.12.1 with R Version 3.3.2 (2016-10-31) on a Linux machine.

Thank you for your help.

