Hi,

I recently ran into a situation that suggested a "clustered" or vector regression model such that:
The dimension of the random vector varies from cluster to cluster,
The covariance matrices of the residuals are structured and depend on a fixed number of parameters.
In existing software, I couldn't find any options that would allow for this, so I turned to Stan. I created a simple simulation in R <https://github.com/baogorek/Miscellaneous/blob/master/R_code/stan/ragged_array_cov_est.R> where there is a structured covariance matrix for the vector (X1, X2, X3) such that:
Var(X1) = Var(X2) = sigma_a^2,
Var(X3) = sigma_b^2,
Cov(X1, X2) = rho1,
Cov(X1, X3) = rho2,
Cov(X2, X3) = rho2,
Where the clustered observations are observed as one of: (X1, X2), (X1, X3), (X2, X3), or (X1, X2, X3).

In my Stan code <https://github.com/baogorek/Miscellaneous/blob/master/R_code/stan/ragged_array_cov_est.stan>, I believe I got it to work by creating a "master" covariance matrix in transformed parameters and then putting sub-segments of the data vector into local variable of fixed size in the model block. The only bad thing is having to "repeat myself" for every vector size:

  for (i in 1:n_clusters) {
    p = subj_upper[i] - subj_lower[i] + 1;
    if (p == 2) {
      mu_2 = rep_vector(0, 2);
      Sigma_2 = Sigma[type[subj_lower[i]:subj_upper[i]],
                      type[subj_lower[i]:subj_upper[i]]];
      y_vec2 = to_vector(y[subj_lower[i]:subj_upper[i]]);
      y_vec2 ~ multi_normal(mu_2, Sigma_2);
    } else if (p == 3) {
      mu_3 = rep_vector(0, 3);
      Sigma_3 = Sigma[type[subj_lower[i]:subj_upper[i]],
                      type[subj_lower[i]:subj_upper[i]]];

      y_vec3 = to_vector(y[subj_lower[i]:subj_upper[i]]);
      y_vec3 ~ multi_normal(mu_3, Sigma_3);
    }
  }

Also, as an inexperienced bayesian practitioner, there are a few additional points that concern me:
My priors don't ensure positive definiteness,
Stan gives a warning message about my "transformation" of the data into the cluster-sized variables,
There seems to be some bias in the posterior draws, but perhaps this is expected
Hopefully this example is interesting to the group. I'm looking for general feedback and better ways to implement this type of model.

Ben

Hello,

I have a model of this kind

y[n] = lognormal(mu_1,sigma_1) * 0.5 + lognormal(mu_2,sigma_2) * 0.1 + lognormal(mu_3,sigma_3) * 0.4

OR

y[n] ~ Density( lognormal(mu_1,sigma_1) * 0.5 + lognormal(mu_2,sigma_2) * 0.1 + lognormal(mu_3,sigma_3) * 0.4, error)

Can I calculate mus and sigmas without calculating the intermediate parameter coming from that distribution?

For example

y[n] ~ Density( theta_1[n] * 0.5 + Theta_2[n] * 0.1 + theta_3[n] * 0.4, error)

theta_1[n] ~ lognormal(mu_1,sigma_1);
...
...

Here my model
data {
    int<lower=1> G;
    int<lower=1> S; // number of cases
    int<lower=1> P; // number of cases
    vector[P] a[S]; // predictor (covariate)
    vector[S] y[G]; // outcome (variate)
    real ex_mean_mean;
    real ex_mean_sd;
}
transformed data{
    vector[P] log_a[S]; // predictor (covariate)
    for(s in 1:S) log_a[s] = log(a[s]);
}
parameters {
    vector<lower=0>[P] log_ex[S,G]; // unknown true value
    vector<lower=0.00001>[P] ex_sd[G]; // prior scale
    vector<lower=0>[P] ex_mean[G]; // prior location
}
model {

    for(g in 1:G){
